1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,944
Welcome! In this video we'll use trigonometry to measure things we can't reach directly: the height of a building, the distance to a ship, and the direction of travel.

2
00:00:09,333 --> 00:00:13,461
We'll cover angles of elevation and depression first, and then bearings.

3
00:00:15,250 --> 00:00:18,978
Imagine you're standing on level ground, looking up at the top of a tower.

4
00:00:19,366 --> 00:00:22,582
First, picture a horizontal line straight out from your eye.

5
00:00:22,966 --> 00:00:25,414
Your line of sight goes up to the top of the tower.

6
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:32,168
The angle between the horizontal and the line of sight is called the angle of elevation. You raise your eyes to see the object.

7
00:00:32,566 --> 00:00:38,454
Remember: an angle of elevation is always measured upward from the horizontal, never from the vertical.

8
00:00:40,250 --> 00:00:43,978
Now you're standing on top of a cliff, looking down at a boat on the sea.

9
00:00:44,366 --> 00:00:49,438
Again, start with the horizontal line from your eye, and draw the line of sight down to the boat.

10
00:00:49,833 --> 00:00:57,241
The angle between them is the angle of depression. You lower your eyes to see the object. It's still measured from the horizontal, but downward.

11
00:00:57,633 --> 00:01:02,001
Here's a useful fact. Someone in the boat looking up at you sees an angle of elevation.

12
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:11,632
Because the two horizontal lines are parallel, these are alternate angles, so they're equal. The angle of depression from the top equals the angle of elevation from the bottom.

13
00:01:13,416 --> 00:01:21,032
Let's try an example. You stand thirty meters from a building. The angle of elevation to the top is forty degrees. How tall is the building?

14
00:01:21,416 --> 00:01:28,312
Sketch a right triangle. The ground distance of thirty meters is the side adjacent to the angle, and the height h is the side opposite.

15
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:34,172
Opposite and adjacent means we use tangent. Tangent of forty degrees equals h over thirty.

16
00:01:34,566 --> 00:01:41,494
Multiply both sides by thirty. h equals thirty times tangent forty, which is about thirty times zero point eight three nine one.

17
00:01:41,883 --> 00:01:44,731
So the building is about twenty five point two meters tall.

18
00:01:46,516 --> 00:01:53,412
From the top of an eighty meter lighthouse, the angle of depression to a boat is twenty five degrees. How far is the boat from the lighthouse?

19
00:01:53,800 --> 00:02:00,280
The angle of depression equals the angle of elevation from the boat, so the angle at the boat is also twenty five degrees.

20
00:02:00,666 --> 00:02:05,642
From the boat's corner, the eighty meter height is opposite, and the distance d is adjacent.

21
00:02:06,033 --> 00:02:09,233
So tangent of twenty five degrees equals eighty over d.

22
00:02:09,633 --> 00:02:16,209
This time d is in the denominator, so rearrange: d equals eighty divided by tangent twenty five degrees.

23
00:02:16,600 --> 00:02:22,264
That's eighty over zero point four six six three, or about one hundred seventy one point six meters.

24
00:02:24,050 --> 00:02:32,242
Sometimes we need the angle itself. A kite is flying on a fifty meter string, thirty five meters above your hand. What is the angle of elevation of the kite?

25
00:02:32,633 --> 00:02:40,169
The thirty five meters is opposite the angle, and the fifty meter string is the hypotenuse. Opposite over hypotenuse means sine.

26
00:02:40,566 --> 00:02:47,366
To undo sine, use the inverse sine button on your calculator. The angle of elevation is about forty four point four degrees.

27
00:02:49,150 --> 00:02:54,462
A bearing describes a direction as an angle. Navigators, pilots, and hikers all use them.

28
00:02:54,850 --> 00:02:59,474
There are three rules for a three figure bearing. One: always start from north.

29
00:02:59,866 --> 00:03:02,186
Two: measure the angle clockwise.

30
00:03:02,583 --> 00:03:08,055
And three: always write it with three digits, so forty five degrees becomes zero four five.

31
00:03:08,450 --> 00:03:13,138
Watch the bearing as the direction turns. North east is zero four five degrees.

32
00:03:13,533 --> 00:03:16,061
Due east is zero nine zero degrees.

33
00:03:16,450 --> 00:03:19,602
South is one eighty, and south west is two two five.

34
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:26,384
West is two seven zero. And a direction just west of north, like here, is three three zero degrees.

35
00:03:26,783 --> 00:03:40,879
You may also see compass bearings, which start from north or south and turn toward east or west. North thirty degrees west is the same as three three zero. South forty degrees east means start at south and turn forty degrees toward east, which is one four zero.

36
00:03:42,666 --> 00:03:47,674
Suppose B is on a bearing of zero six zero degrees from A. What is the bearing of A from B?

37
00:03:48,066 --> 00:03:52,450
Draw a new north line at B, and turn clockwise from it until you face A.

38
00:03:52,850 --> 00:04:00,338
Facing the opposite way is a half turn, so the back bearing is the bearing plus one hundred eighty degrees. Sixty plus one eighty is two four zero.

39
00:04:00,733 --> 00:04:07,965
If the original bearing is already more than one eighty, subtract one eighty instead, so the answer stays between zero and three sixty.

40
00:04:09,750 --> 00:04:18,758
Bearings combine with right triangles too. A ship sails fifty kilometers on a bearing of zero six zero. How far east, and how far north, has it travelled?

41
00:04:19,150 --> 00:04:24,494
Drop a vertical line from the ship to make a right triangle. The sixty degree angle is measured from north.

42
00:04:24,883 --> 00:04:31,555
The east distance is opposite the sixty degree angle, so it's fifty times sine sixty, about forty three point three kilometers.

43
00:04:31,950 --> 00:04:38,142
The north distance is adjacent, so it's fifty times cosine sixty, which is exactly twenty five kilometers.

44
00:04:39,933 --> 00:04:51,837
Finally, a two leg journey. A boat sails twelve kilometers from A to B on a bearing of zero four zero, then nine kilometers from B to C on a bearing of one three zero. How far is C from A, and on what bearing?

45
00:04:52,233 --> 00:05:04,985
First, find the angle at B. The back bearing of A from B is two two zero degrees. The boat leaves B on one three zero, so the angle between the two legs is two twenty minus one thirty: ninety degrees. A right angle!

46
00:05:05,383 --> 00:05:15,431
With a right angle, Pythagoras gives the distance. A C is the square root of twelve squared plus nine squared, which is the square root of two hundred twenty five: fifteen kilometers.

47
00:05:15,816 --> 00:05:23,336
For the direction, find angle alpha at A. Tangent alpha is nine over twelve, so alpha is about thirty six point nine degrees.

48
00:05:23,733 --> 00:05:29,205
Add this to the first bearing. The bearing of C from A is about zero seven six point nine degrees.

49
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:34,456
To recap. An angle of elevation is measured up from the horizontal.

50
00:05:34,850 --> 00:05:37,714
An angle of depression is measured down from the horizontal.

51
00:05:38,100 --> 00:05:42,292
The angle of depression from the top equals the angle of elevation from the bottom.

52
00:05:42,683 --> 00:05:46,235
Bearings are measured from north, clockwise, and written with three digits.

53
00:05:46,633 --> 00:05:56,105
And a back bearing is found by adding or subtracting one hundred eighty degrees. In every problem, sketch the right triangle first, then choose sine, cosine, or tangent.

54
00:05:57,100 --> 00:05:58,172
Thanks for watching!

